WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.467
This slide helps us see more clearly how a generator works.

00:00:03.467 --> 00:00:05.627
We have a countdown function.

00:00:05.627 --> 00:00:10.167
The function starts with n, and as long as n is greater than zero,

00:00:10.167 --> 00:00:14.467
it yields the current value and then decreases n by one.

00:00:14.467 --> 00:00:21.617
Notice that when we write g equals countdown three, Python does not immediately run the entire countdown.

00:00:21.617 --> 00:00:25.017
Instead, g becomes a generator object.

00:00:25.017 --> 00:00:26.957
Then we call next g.

00:00:26.957 --> 00:00:30.357
The first call starts the function and gives us three.

00:00:30.357 --> 00:00:33.542
The generator pauses at the yield statement.

00:00:33.542 --> 00:00:38.537
When we call next again, the function continues from that exact location.

00:00:38.537 --> 00:00:40.122
It decreases n and eventually yields two.

00:00:40.122 --> 00:00:46.697
This is one of the most important things to understand about generators.

00:00:46.697 --> 00:00:49.777
They maintain their state between requests.

00:00:49.777 --> 00:00:52.652
Run the example and look at the two values.

00:00:52.652 --> 00:00:56.982
Then, if you want to experiment, add another print next g.

00:00:56.982 --> 00:00:58.732
You should get one.

00:00:58.732 --> 00:01:05.087
If you call next one more time after the generator is finished, Python will raise StopIteration.

00:01:05.087 --> 00:01:08.991
That simply means that the generator has no more values to produce.
